  <Section {...S.formatting}>
        <p>These classes handle truncation, line clamping, text wrapping, opacity, unstyled lists and hover effects.</p>

        <h5>Truncation</h5>
        <p>
          To truncate a long line of text with an ellipsis, add the class
          <code>truncate</code> to the element that contains it. The example below truncates a heading inside a card.
          A flex or grid child may also need <code>.min-w-0</code> to shrink.
        </p>
        <div class="row">
          <div class="s6 offset-s3">
            <article>
              <h4 class="truncate">This is an extremely long title that will be truncated</h4>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<h4 class="truncate">This is an extremely long title that will be truncated</h4>
`} />

        <h5>Line clamp</h5>
        <p>
          To cut text to a few lines and end it in an ellipsis, add <code>line-clamp-1</code>,
          <code>line-clamp-2</code> or <code>line-clamp-3</code>. <code>line-clamp-none</code> shows every line
          again, so <code>line-clamp-2 expanded:line-clamp-none</code> clamps on compact and medium windows only.
          The classes set <code>display</code>, so put them on a child of a flex or grid container, not on the
          container. Pad a wrapper rather than the clamped element, because block-end padding shows the top of the
          next line.
        </p>
        <div class="row">
          <div class="s6 offset-s3">
            <article>
              <p class="line-clamp-2">This card description runs on for several lines, but the class cuts it after the second line and ends that line in an ellipsis, so cards in a row keep the same height.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<p class="line-clamp-2">This card description runs on for several lines…</p>
`} />

        <h5>Text wrapping</h5>
        <table class="striped">
          <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>.break-words</code></td><td><code>overflow-wrap: anywhere</code> lets long URLs or identifiers wrap within their container.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.text-nowrap</code></td><td><code>white-space: nowrap</code> keeps text on one line without clipping or ellipsis.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.text-wrap</code></td><td><code>white-space: normal</code> restores ordinary wrapping.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.text-balance</code></td><td><code>text-wrap-style: balance</code> evens out the line lengths of a short block, such as a card title. Headings already do this.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.text-pretty</code></td><td><code>text-wrap-style: pretty</code> avoids a single short word on the last line. Paragraphs already do this.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Allow wrapping on narrow screens when keeping a line intact would hide required text. These classes do not change display or overflow.</p>

        <h5>Opacity</h5>
        <p>
          <code>.opacity-0</code>, <code>.opacity-25</code>, <code>.opacity-38</code>, <code>.opacity-50</code>,
          <code>.opacity-75</code> and <code>.opacity-100</code> set <code>opacity</code> to 0, 0.25, 0.38, 0.5, 0.75 and 1.
          38 is the M3 opacity for disabled content. A class overrides a component's own opacity, and unlayered page CSS overrides the class.
        </p>
        <p>
          Dimmed text still needs enough contrast unless it belongs to a disabled control. Opacity disables and hides nothing:
          even at <code>.opacity-0</code> the element takes clicks and focus and stays in the accessibility tree.
          Use <code>disabled</code>, <code>.hide</code> or <code>hidden</code> for that.
        </p>
        <div class="flex gap-3" aria-hidden="true">
          {[25, 38, 50, 75, 100].map(step => <div class={`primary corner-medium opacity-${step}`} style="width: 48px; height: 48px"></div>)}
        </div>
<Code code={`
<img class="opacity-50" src="pattern.svg" alt="">
`} />

        <h5>Unstyled lists</h5>
        <p>
          Add <code>list-unstyled</code> to a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> to remove its markers
          and its inline-start padding. The list keeps its margins, and a list nested inside it keeps its own markers.
          WebKit drops the list role from a list without markers, so add <code>role="list"</code> as well. The
          semantics checks flag a <code>.list-unstyled</code> list with no <code>role</code>; a list that sets another
          role on purpose, such as <code>role="none"</code>, passes.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-unstyled" role="list">
          <li>Invoices</li>
          <li>Receipts</li>
        </ul>
<Code code={`
<ul class="list-unstyled" role="list">
  <li>Invoices</li>
  <li>Receipts</li>
</ul>
`} />


        <h5>Hover</h5>
        <p>
          Add <code>hoverable</code> to animate the box shadow, as in the example below. It works
          on most elements, but it is meant for cards.
        </p>
        <article class="hoverable">Hoverable article</article>
<Code code={`
<article class="hoverable">Hoverable article</article>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.pointer}>
        <table class="striped">
          <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Declaration</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>.pointer-events-none</code></td><td><code>pointer-events: none</code>. Clicks, taps and hovers on the element and its children reach whatever lies beneath it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.pointer-events-auto</code></td><td><code>pointer-events: auto</code>. A child of a <code>.pointer-events-none</code> element takes pointer input again.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.cursor-pointer</code></td><td><code>cursor: pointer</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
<Code code={`
<div class="relative">
  <a href="/photos/42"><img class="w-full" src="..." alt="Fishing boats moored in a harbor at dusk"></a>
  <div class="pointer-events-none flex align-end justify-between p-2" style="position: absolute; inset: 0">
    <span class="label-large">Photo by Ana Ruiz</span>
    <button type="button" class="pointer-events-auto">Hide caption</button>
  </div>
</div>

<tr class="cursor-pointer">
  <td><a href="/orders/42">Order 42</a></td>
  <td>Shipped</td>
</tr>
`} />
        <p>
          A click on the caption opens the photo link beneath it, and the button still takes
          its own clicks. <code>.pointer-events-none</code> stops the pointer only: the element
          and its controls stay in the tab order and still respond to keys. To disable a control,
          use <code>disabled</code>; to take a whole region out of use, use <code>inert</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>.cursor-pointer</code> changes the cursor and nothing else. Links and buttons
          already show it. In the table row above, a page script opens the row's link on a click
          anywhere in the row, and keyboard users reach the same link by Tab. An element that
          responds to clicks but isn't a link or button still needs a role, a tab stop and key
          handling, so use a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> where one fits.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["browser-default"]}>
        <p>
          ExpressiveCSS overrides many default browser styles. Add the
          <code>.browser-default</code> class to an element to opt it out of those styles.
        </p>
        <table class="striped">
